"Stripe Press — Maintenance (https://press.stripe.com/maintenance-part-one)\nciteturn639view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn637view0\",\"id\":1}); Total lines: 3390\nL0: Maintenance: Of Everything, Part One\nL1: \nL2: Stewart Brand\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Living cover†stripe.press】\nL5: The first in a multi-volume work, Maintenance: Of Everything, Part One offers a comprehensive overview of the civilizational importance of maintenance. The book explores the insights that can be gleaned from the maintenance of sailboats, vehicles, and weapons, with absorbing detours into the evolution of precision in manufacturing, the enduring importance of manuals, sustainment in the military, and the never-ending battle against corrosion.\nL6: Maintenance: Of Everything is a wide-ranging and provocative call to expand what we mean by “maintenance.” It invites us to understand not only the profound impact maintenance has on our daily lives but also why taking responsibility for maintaining something—whether a motorcycle, a monument, or our planet—can be a radical act.\nL7: 【1†Purchase on Bookshop    $40†bookshop.org】 【2†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $40†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【3†Purchase on Amazon    $40†www.amazon.com】\nL8: \nL9: Author\nL10: Stewart Brand is the cofounder and president of The Long Now Foundation. He created and edited the National Book Award-winning Whole Earth Catalog from 1968 to 1998. His books include The Media Lab (1987), How Buildings Learn (1994), The Clock of the Long Now (1999), and Whole Earth Discipline (2009). He was the subject of the documentary We Are As Gods (2020).\nL11: \nL12: 【4†Twitter†x.com】\nL13: \nL14: 【5†Website†sb.longnow.org】\nL15: \nL16: Praise\nL17: > Stewart Brand makes a persuasive case that keeping the human show on the road through well-planned maintenance is as vital and as fascinating a task as innovation and discovery themselves. A deliciously good book.\nL18: \nL19: Matt Ridley\nL20: \nL21: author of The Rational Optimist\nL22: > Once again, Stewart Brand reframes our worldview with a new perspective. You may not imagine you would be interested in rust, Soviet tanks, or tricked-out Model Ts—that is, until Brand reexamines them through the lens of maintenance. Maintenance: Of Everything is destined to be a classic.\nL23: \nL24: Danny Hillis\nL25: \nL26: cofounder of Applied Invention\nL27: > Stewart Brand is back with a manifesto on maintenance, the tool that empowers all tools. Preventative maintenance, deferred maintenance, and emergency maintenance: this much-needed, no-nonsense treatise illuminates the difference, and why it counts.\nL28: \nL29: George Dyson\nL30: \nL31: author of Darwin Among the Machines and Turing’s Cathedral\nL32: \nL33: > Civilization is maintenance. No one else but Stewart Brand is talking about the art and science of maintenance and how to do it well. This will be an instant classic.\nL34: \nL35: Kevin Kelly\nL36: founding executive editor of Wired\nL37: \nL38: > Brand masterfully takes us on a journey through the world of the fixers keeping the gears of civilization rolling. If you’re the kind of person who wants to know why things break and how to stop it, you should read this book.\nL39: \nL40: Kyle Wiens\nL41: \nL42: CEO of iFixit\nL43: \nL44: > What a splendid book to read while fixing an old house. I stopped moaning and whining and became a quiet hero of relentless “sustainment.”\nL45: \nL46: Bruce Sterling\nL47: \nL48: author and editor of Mirrorshades: The Cyberpunk Anthology\nL49: > Maintenance is interesting in more ways than I can count. I felt smarter for having read this book and lucky for being alive at a time when Stewart Brand is writing it.\nL50: \nL51: Charles C. Mann\nL52: \nL53: author of 1491: New Revelations of the Americas Before Columbus\nL54: > In his fresh and inimitable prose, Stewart Brand makes a striking case for what he calls maintenance–the upkeep of the things that make modern life possible. Back to the basics of caring and repairing–sailing boats, automobiles, sewing machines, Colt revolvers, bicycles and even tanks in the Yom Kippur war–Brand shows us why know-how and understanding how machines work really matter.\nL55: New things get our attention but mastering how to take care of things we already have is life work that counts like no other. This book is Stewart Brand at his best.\nL56: Jerry Brown\nL57: \nL58: former Governor of California\nL59: \nL60: > A new classic — original and fresh, smart, wise, a universal topic, Stewart makes me so happy once again.\nL61: \nL62: Edward Tufte\nL63: \nL64: statistician and professor emeritus of political science, statistics, and computer science at Yale University\nL65: \nL66: Nomad Letters: The Full Collection of the Nomad Investment Partnership Letters to Partners, 2001–2014\nL67: \nL68: Nick Sleep\nL69: Between 2001 and 2014, amid gale-force global economic headwinds, Nick Sleep and Qais Zakaria’s Nomad Investment Partnership dramatically outperformed the market—and their biannual Letters to Partners earned cult status as some of the most clear-headed writing on investing and deciphering market forces. Nomad Letters is the first compilation of these letters in print, sharing the enduring insights of two of the sharpest minds in value investing.\nL70: Featuring a new prologue by Nick Sleep and a foreword by Stripe cofounder and president John Collison, Nomad Letters details the investment philosophy behind Sleep and Zakaria’s success—an approach that favors patience, conviction, and dedication to long-term competitive advantage over short-term returns.\nL71: It includes Sleep’s reflections on the pioneering concept of “scale economics shared,” embodied in Nomad’s longtime investments in Costco and Amazon, and on the pair’s profound commitment to long-term thinking, never following the herd, and pursuing a meaningful life beyond wealth accumulation. Not only a blueprint for investors, Nomad Letters is essential reading for anyone seeking to build long-lasting businesses, understand market dynamics, and see the world more clearly.\nL72: Preorder from Stripe Press\nL73: \nL74: 【6†Preorder on Stripe    $40†buy.stripe.com】\nL75: \nL76: Preorder elsewhere\nL77: \nL78: 【7†Preorder on Bookshop    $40†bookshop.org】 【8†Preorder on Barnes & Noble    $40†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【9†Preorder on Amazon    $40†www.amazon.com】\nL79: \nL80: Author\nL81: \nL82: Nick Sleep cofounded and co-managed the Nomad Investment Partnership with Qais Zakaria from 2001 to 2014. He continues to invest privately and through his charitable foundation, the IGY Foundation.\nL83: \nL84: Praise\nL85: > There are three sources of competitive advantage in investing: information, analysis, and behaviour. Nick Sleep and Qais Zakaria were superb analysts, but it was their patient temperament and long-term orientation that really set them apart. These letters offer a masterclass in value investing.\nL86: \nL87: Bill Miller\nL88: \nL89: Founder, Miller Value Partners\nL90: > This is a wonderful reminder that there are plenty of good things in life which compound in value over time, yet which never appear on a balance sheet. In many ways, the quest to identify and foster these forces is perhaps the most worthwhile use of our time on this planet.\nL91: \nL92: Rory Sutherland\nL93: \nL94: Vice chairman, Ogilvy UK\nL95: > The Nomad Letters belong in rarefied company, alongside the shareholder letters of Warren Buffett. Like him, Nick Sleep points us toward a form of enlightened capitalism characterized by virtues like patience, rationality, honesty, humility, prudence, and giving back. This is capitalism done right.\nL96: \nL97: William Green\nL98: \nL99: Author of Richer, Wiser, Happier\nL100: > There are three bodies of investment writing that have truly defined how I see business, investing, and life. The Nomad Letters are one of them. Gathered here in book form, they now have the lasting place they deserve on the shelves of serious readers everywhere.\nL101: \nL102: Christopher Begg\nL103: \nL104: Cofounder, East Coast Asset Management\nL105: \nL106: Built to Grow: A Handbook for High-Performance Sales Teams\nL107: \nL108: Stephanie Friedman\nL109: Startup founders often wish for a playbook for building and scaling a sales function. All too often, it’s an experimental, improvisational process, in which any wrong move can lead to wasted time and capital. But in her 20 years building and scaling go-to-market teams, Stephanie Friedman has identified a set of steps that any B2B founder can take to build a successful sales organization.\nL110: This book is that playbook—a structured and repeatable methodology for building a high-performing sales function from a company’s earliest days. In Built to Grow, Friedman lays out a systematic framework for building and managing a sales organization, from defining the repeatable sales motion and creating a sales playbook to hiring the right salespeople and managing the revenue engine.\nL111: The book is divided into three phases of startup growth—Experiment, Build, and Scale—and offers actionable insights and tactics drawn from Friedman’s two decades of experience working with successful startups, both as an operator and as a board director and adviser to founders and CEOs. Built to Grow is the go-to-market field guide for founders looking to scale from $0 to $100 million in annual sales, which often coincides with building the sales team from 0 to 100 salespeople.\nL112: The principles put forward in Built to Grow allow startup founders and CEOs to implement a repeatable and predictable approach to selling their product and building a generational company from day one.\nL113: 【10†Purchase on Bookshop    $40†bookshop.org】 【11†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $40†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【12†Purchase on Amazon    $40†www.amazon.com】\nL114: \nL115: Author\nL116: Stephanie Friedman is an angel investor, advisor, and startup executive with over 20 years of go-to-market operations experience, with a focus on enterprise software. Previously, she helped build three successful startups as an operator and revenue leader, and after one acquisition stayed on for two years to manage sales operations for a $380 million business as part of a public company.\nL117: She joined her last startup, Xamarin, in 2012 as their first non-engineering hire and built the customer-facing organization from zero to 100 sales and customer success people and from $0 to almost $50 million in run rate sales in less than four years. Xamarin was acquired by Microsoft in 2016. She has lived and worked in six countries, is fluent in five languages, and holds a master’s degree in international business.\nL118: She is also the founder of Love Dogs Rescue and Foundation and she lives in California with her husband, her two children, and (currently) two rescue dogs.\nL119: 【13†Twitter†x.com】\nL120: \nL121: Praise\nL122: \nL123: > Successfully scaling a company means building intentional, repeatable mechanisms that grow with your business. In Built to Grow, Stephanie Friedman has written the definitive guide to replacing start-up ambiguity with a rigorous, predictable revenue engine. If you need to build a high-performing go-to-market organization, this is the book for you.\nL124: \nL125: Claire Hughes Johnson\nL126: \nL127: Corporate officer and advisor at Stripe; author of Scaling People\nL128: > In the world of enterprise software, trying to scale without a bulletproof go-to-market playbook is a recipe for disaster. Fortunately, Stephanie Friedman’s Built to Grow demystifies the process and tells you what you need to know about hiring your early sales team, forecasting, managing a sales pipeline, and creating a sales playbook—in short developing a world-class customer-facing function. Anyone building a B2B business should read this book.\nL129: \nL130: Aaron Levie\nL131: \nL132: CEO at Box\nL133: > Stephanie Friedman has written an indispensable companion for founders who must turn the first spark of sales into an organization built to endure and grow. With clarity and pragmatism, she shows how to choose the right people, follow the right processes, measure what truly matters, and unite those who build the product with those who carry its promise into the world.\nL134: \nL135: Daniel Gross\nL136: \nL137: Entrepreneur and investor\nL138: > A great product isn’t enough. You also have to build the machine that consistently sells it. In Built to Grow, Stephanie Friedman offers founders a specific framework on how to develop a repeatable sales motion, hire a high-performing sales and customer success team and build a revenue engine they can count on. This is where scaling actually happens.\nL139: \nL140: Ravi Gupta\nL141: \nL142: Partner, Sequoia Capital\nL143: > When you’re building a business, the shift away from founder-led sales is inevitable, pivotal, and difficult. Stephanie Friedman’s new book is exactly the kind of actionable roadmap you need to get this transition right—and to create a virtuous circle of customer success and sustainable revenue growth.\nL144: \nL145: Christina Cacioppo\nL146: \nL147: Co-founder and CEO, Vanta\nL148: \nL149: Poor Charlie’s Almanack: The Essential Wit and Wisdom of Charles T. Munger\nL150: \nL151: Edited by Peter D. Kaufman\nL152: \nL153: 【14†Website†stripe.press】\nL154: 【15†Living cover†stripe.press】\nL155: “Spend each day trying to be a little wiser than you were when you woke up,” Charlie Munger advises in Poor Charlie’s Almanack. Originally published in 2005, this compilation of 11 talks by the legendary Berkshire Hathaway vice-chairman has become a touchstone for a generation of investors and entrepreneurs.\nL156: Delivered with Munger’s characteristic rhetorical flair, Poor Charlie’s Almanack draws on his encyclopedic knowledge of business, finance, history, philosophy, physics, and ethics to introduce the latticework of mental models that underpin his rational and rigorous approach to life, learning, and decision-making. It is an essential volume for any reader seeking to go to bed a little wiser than when they woke up. This abridged edition features a new foreword by Stripe cofounder and president John Collison.\nL157: 【16†Purchase on Stripe    $30†buy.stripe.com】 【17†Purchase on Bookshop    $30†bookshop.org】 【18†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $30†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【19†Purchase on Amazon    $30†www.amazon.com】 【20†Now available in softcover    $18†www.amazon.com】\nL158: \nL159: Authors\nL160: Charles T. Munger (1924–2023) was an investor, businessman, and former real estate attorney. He was the vice-chairman of Berkshire Hathaway, the multinational conglomerate controlled by Warren Buffett. He was chairman of Wesco Financial Corporation from 1984 to 2011. He was also chairman of the Daily Journal Corporation and a director of Costco Wholesale Corporation.\nL161: Peter D. Kaufman is the chairman and CEO of Glenair, Inc. He was a longtime director of Wesco Financial Corporation and is a former director of Daily Journal Corporation. In 2005, he edited and published Poor Charlie’s Almanack, a compilation of talks given by his longtime friend Charlie Munger.\nL162: 【21†PDF†assets.stripeassets.com】\nL163: \nL164: Praise\nL165: \nL166: > This book is something of a publishing miracle—never advertised, yet year after year selling many thousands of copies from its internet site.\nL167: \nL168: 【22†Warren Buffett†www.poorcharliesalmanack.com】\nL169: \nL170: CEO of Berkshire Hathaway\nL171: > Charlie Munger was born before corporate speak, before business leaders minced words to obscure truths. But Charlie Munger’s words have always cut like a knife—and that sharp spirit of truth-seeking built Berkshire into what it is today. We should all aspire to such clarity.\nL172: \nL173: Katherine Boyle\nL174: \nL175: general partner at a16z\nL176: \nL177: > Poor Charlie’s Almanack is a timeless classic that will change how you approach life. There is a billion-dollar education inside this book.\nL178: \nL179: Shane Parrish\nL180: founder of Syrus Partners and Farnam Street\nL181: \nL182: > This book is the best way to learn from Munger and his ideas. It’s a must-read for anyone interested in thinking clearly, rationally, and effectively.\nL183: \nL184: Ana Lorena Fabrega\nL185: \nL186: author, educator, and chief evangelist at Synthesis\nL187: \nL188: > Charlie Munger’s insights are timeless. Some of these speeches were delivered over 20 years ago, but they feel like they could have been given yesterday.\nL189: \nL190: Tyler Hogge\nL191: \nL192: venture partner at Pelion\nL193: > If I could only read one book to help me succeed in life and business, this is the book I’d read. Charlie’s timeless wisdom has inspired millions and will continue to resonate for generations to come.\nL194: \nL195: David Senra\nL196: \nL197: creator of the Founders podcast\nL198: \nL199: The Origins of Efficiency\nL200: \nL201: Brian Potter\nL202: Efficiency is the engine of civilization. But where do improvements in production efficiency come from? In The Origins of Efficiency, Brian Potter argues that improving production efficiency—finding ways to produce goods and services in less time, with less labor, using fewer resources—is the force behind some of the most consequential changes in human history.\nL203: He examines the fundamental characteristics of a production process and how each can be made faster, cheaper, and more reliable, with detailed examples from a range of industries: steel and semiconductors, wind turbines and container shipping, Tesla and the Ford Model T, and more.\nL204: The Origins of Efficiency is a comprehensive companion for anyone seeking to understand how we arrived at this age of material abundance—and how we can push efficiency improvements into domains like housing, medicine, and education, where much work is left to be done.\nL205: 【23†Purchase on Bookshop    $40†bookshop.org】 【24†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $40†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【25†Purchase on Amazon    $40†www.amazon.com】\nL206: \nL207: Author\nL208: \nL209: Brian Potter is the author of the Construction Physics newsletter and a senior infrastructure fellow at the Institute for Progress. He previously managed an engineering team at Katerra, a SoftBank-backed construction startup, and has 15 years of experience as a structural engineer.\nL210: \nL211: 【26†Twitter†x.com】\nL212: \nL213: 【27†Website†www.construction-physics.com】\nL214: 【28†PDF†assets.stripeassets.com】\nL215: \nL216: Praise\nL217: \nL218: > Brilliant and comprehensive. Brian Potter is the single best writer in America on how important things get built, and his new book is the single best example of his work.\nL219: \nL220: Derek Thompson\nL221: \nL222: author of Hit Makers and coauthor of Abundance\nL223: > A whole-systems overview of all known manufacturing processes (past and present), crammed with dense details, stories, anecdotes, and lessons from the front lines… If you have any interest in, or responsibility for, how to make stuff at scale, you need to read this book.\nL224: \nL225: Kevin Kelly\nL226: \nL227: founding executive editor of Wired\nL228: > Brian Potter does impeccable, detailed research that he turns into clairvoyant insight about civilization’s most arcane processes. Here, he examines the mechanisms that enable spectacular efficiency in the production of goods and services.\nL229: \nL230: Stewart Brand\nL231: \nL232: cofounder and president of The Long Now Foundation\nL233: \nL234: > America has forgotten how to make things. Brian Potter is here to help us remember. By the time you finish this book, you’ll feel like you’re well on your way to becoming an expert in manufacturing.\nL235: Noah Smith\nL236: \nL237: author of Noahpinion\nL238: \nL239: The Scaling Era: An Oral History of AI, 2019–2025\nL240: \nL241: Dwarkesh Patel with Gavin Leech\nL242: \nL243: 【29†Living cover†stripe.press】\nL244: How did we build large language models? How do they think, if they think? What will the world look like if we have billions of AIs that are as smart as humans, or even smarter? In a series of in-depth interviews with leading AI researchers and company founders—including Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei, DeepMind cofounder Demis Hassabis, OpenAI cofounder Ilya Sutskever, MIRI cofounder Eliezer Yudkowsky, and Meta CEO Mark Zuckerberg—Dwarkesh Patel provides the first comprehensive and contemporary portrait of the technology that is transforming our world.\nL245: Drawn from his interviews on the Dwarkesh Podcast, these curated excerpts range from the technical details of how LLMs work to the possibility of an AI takeover or explosive economic growth. It also includes 170+ definitions and visualizations, classic essays on the theme, and previously unpublished interviews. The Scaling Era offers readers unprecedented insight into a transformative moment in AI’s development—and a vision of what comes next.\nL246: 【30†Purchase on Bookshop    $35†bookshop.org】 【31†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $35†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【32†Purchase on Amazon    $35†www.amazon.com】\nL247: \nL248: Authors\nL249: \nL250: Dwarkesh Patel is the host of Dwarkesh Podcast, where he produces deeply researched interviews with both obscure intellectuals and the most influential figures of our time.\nL251: \nL252: 【33†Twitter†x.com】\nL253: \nL254: 【34†Website†www.dwarkesh.com】\nL255: Gavin Leech is the cofounder of Arb Research, a consultancy that does empirical and conceptual work in various sciences. He has a PhD in AI.\nL256: \nL257: 【35†Twitter†x.com】\nL258: \nL259: 【36†Website†www.gleech.org】\nL260: \nL261: 【37†PDF†assets.stripeassets.com】\nL262: \nL263: Praise\nL264: > Dwarkesh has done a whole series of excellent video interviews with top AI researchers and engineers, and this book is a great distillation of those conversations to help people understand modern AI models, how they are built, where they are headed, and why they are important.\nL265: \nL266: Jeff Dean\nL267: \nL268: chief scientist at Google DeepMind and Google Research\nL269: > There’s only one test relevant for an artifact about AI: Would you learn more from reading it than you would from using a newly released LLM for the equivalent amount of time? The Scaling Era handily passes.\nL270: \nL271: Patrick McKenzie\nL272: \nL273: author of Bits About Money and host of Complex Systems\nL274: \nL275: > Dwarkesh’s interviews are full of quotes that will be in history books.\nL276: \nL277: Byrne Hobart\nL278: \nL279: investor, consultant, and author of The Diff and Boom\nL280: \nL281: Boom: Bubbles and the End of Stagnation\nL282: \nL283: Byrne Hobart and Tobias Huber\nL284: 【38†Living cover†stripe.press】\nL285: From the Moon landing to the dawning of the atomic age, the decades prior to the 1970s were characterized by the routine invention of transformative technologies at breakneck speed. By comparison, ours is an age of stagnation; of slowing median wage growth, rising inequality, and decelerated scientific discovery. In Boom, Byrne Hobart and Tobias Huber take an inductive approach to this problem.\nL286: They track some of the most significant breakthroughs of the past 100 years—from the Manhattan Project and the Apollo program to Moore’s law and Bitcoin—and reverse-engineer how transformative progress arises from the same dynamics that govern financial bubbles, bringing together small groups with a unified vision, vast funding, and surprisingly poor accountability. Bubbles, they conclude, aren’t all bad—in fact, they create the ideal conditions for transformative innovation.\nL287: Integrating insights from economics, philosophy, and history, Boom provides a blueprint for accelerating innovation and a path to unleash a new era of global prosperity.\nL288: 【39†Purchase on Bookshop    $35†bookshop.org】 【40†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $35†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【41†Purchase on Amazon    $35†www.amazon.com】\nL289: \nL290: Authors\nL291: \nL292: Byrne Hobart is an investor, consultant, and writer. He is the author of The Diff, a daily newsletter covering inflection points in finance and technology. He is also a partner at Anomaly, a frontier tech investment firm.\nL293: \nL294: 【42†Twitter†x.com】\nL295: \nL296: 【43†Website†www.thediff.co】\nL297: Tobias Huber is a writer and investor. He is a partner at Anomaly, a frontier tech investment firm. He has a background in philosophy and holds a doctor of science degree from ETH Zurich.\nL298: \nL299: 【44†Twitter†x.com】\nL300: \nL301: 【45†Website†www.tobiashuber.xyz】\nL302: \nL303: 【46†PDF†assets.stripeassets.com】\nL304: \nL305: Praise\nL306: > The dot-com bubble looked like the peak of delusion, but the truly deluded were those who wanted to indefinitely defer the future. Everyone knows bubbles can disguise madness as wisdom; read this book for the alternative history of our age.\nL307: \nL308: Peter Thiel\nL309: \nL310: investor and author of Zero to One\nL311: \nL312: > Boom makes the case that humanity’s greatest risk is not climate change or misaligned superintelligent Al but not making enough progress. A must-read for those who seek to build the future.\nL313: \nL314: Marc Andreessen\nL315: cofounder of Netscape and Andreessen Horowitz\nL316: \nL317: > When is it that temporary bubbles can help drive forward progress? This happened with the railroads, with the internet, and it is likely to happen with AI. Byrne Hobart and Tobias Huber have produced some essential reading on a very important and also understudied topic.\nL318: \nL319: Tyler Cowen\nL320: \nL321: author of The Great Stagnation and professor of economics at George Mason University\nL322: \nL323: Scaling People: Tactics for Management and Company Building\nL324: \nL325: Claire Hughes Johnson\nL326: 【47†Workbooks】\nL327: \nL328: 【48†Interviews】\nL329: Scaling People is a practical and empathetic guide to company building and scaling the most important resource a company has: its people. Drawing on nearly two decades of experience as a Google and Stripe executive, Claire Hughes Johnson offers actionable insights and tactical guidance on everything from crafting foundational documents to hiring and team development to feedback and performance mechanisms.\nL330: The book includes over 100 pages of worksheets, templates, exercises, and example documents to help founders, leaders, and company builders create scalable operating systems and lightweight processes that really work. With Scaling People, Hughes Johnson shares a detailed roadmap for building a sustainable, scalable company—one that’s set up for generational success.\nL331: 【49†Purchase on Bookshop    $35†bookshop.org】 【50†Purchase on Amazon    $35†www.amazon.com】\nL332: \nL333: Author\nL334: \nL335: 【51†Image†images.stripeassets.com】\nL336: Claire Hughes Johnson is a corporate officer and adviser at Stripe. Previously, she was chief operating officer at Stripe from 2014 to 2021, helping the company grow from fewer than 200 employees to more than 7,000. Prior to Stripe, she spent 10 years at Google leading business teams, including overseeing aspects of Gmail, Google Apps, and consumer operations, as well as serving as a vice president for AdWords, Google Offers, and Google’s self-driving car project.\nL337: She serves on the boards of Ameresco, The Atlantic, Aurora Innovation, and HubSpot. She lives outside of Boston with her husband, two children, and two neurotic dachshunds.\nL338: 【52†Twitter†x.com】\nL339: \nL340: Praise\nL341: \nL342: > Whether you are a new manager or a CEO, there are going to be moments when you feel alone and need help. Odds are, the advice you need is in Scaling People. You are going to pull this book off your shelf over and over!\nL343: \nL344: Kim Scott\nL345: \nL346: author of Radical Candor and Just Work\nL347: > There’s a lot of reinventing the wheel in a startup environment. Scaling People offers strong frameworks, sound advice, and tried-and-true processes that any company can use to build stronger foundations and sustainable success.\nL348: \nL349: Reid Hoffman\nL350: \nL351: cofounder of LinkedIn, partner at Greylock, host of Masters of Scale\nL352: > Scaling People is approachable, entertaining, and exceptionally useful, with lots of heart and humor. Claire leverages her years of experience to provide a deeply helpful resource to read and revisit again and again.\nL353: \nL354: Aileen Lee\nL355: \nL356: founder and managing partner of Cowboy Ventures\nL357: > Everything you need to know about building and scaling a company, being an exceptional leader and manager, and getting the best out of your people in one empathetic and enjoyable package—with easy-to-use worksheets and templates to boot.\nL358: \nL359: Frances Frei\nL360: \nL361: author of Uncommon Service and Unleashed, professor at Harvard Business School\nL362: > Claire Hughes Johnson is the ultimate operator. Scaling People proves that it’s possible to operate in a high-growth environment while simultaneously focusing on and caring about developing people—and that doing so also leads to a fundamentally stronger business.\nL363: \nL364: Mallun Yen\nL365: \nL366: founder of Operator Collective\nL367: > I was excited to learn that Claire Hughes Johnson has taken her many years of operating excellence from Stripe and Google and distilled it into a new book. Scaling People is a key book to be added to the shelf of any people leader and manager.\nL368: \nL369: Elad Gil\nL370: \nL371: entrepreneur, investor, and author of High Growth Handbook\nL372: > The world expects founders to succeed and thrive as their company grows. Yet no one takes the time to give them the skills and processes necessary to achieve great leadership. Now someone has—hats off to Claire Johnson for writing Scaling People.\nL373: \nL374: Bill Gurley\nL375: \nL376: general partner at Benchmark\nL377: \nL378: > If you want to scale your business from 10 people to 10,000 without losing the things that make it special, Scaling People is the handbook you need.\nL379: \nL380: Ravi Gupta\nL381: former CFO and COO of Instacart, general partner at Sequoia Capital\nL382: \nL383: > Claire has demystified how you run a large, high-performance organization, with clear concepts and practical templates that you can start implementing today.\nL384: \nL385: Jason Citron\nL386: \nL387: founder and CEO of Discord\nL388: \nL389: Pieces of the Action\nL390: \nL391: Vannevar Bush\nL392: In Pieces of the Action, Vannevar Bush—engineer, inventor, educator, and public face of government-funded science—offers an inside account of one of the most innovative research and development ecosystems of the 20th century.\nL393: As the architect and administrator of an R&D pipeline that efficiently coordinated the work of civilian scientists and the military during World War II, he was central to catalyzing the development of radar and the proximity fuze, the mass production of penicillin, and the initiation of the Manhattan Project.\nL394: Pieces of the Action offers his hard-won lessons on how to operate and manage effectively within complex organizations, build bridges between people and disciplines, and drive ambitious, unprecedented programs to fruition.\nL395: Originally published in 1970, this updated edition includes a foreword from Ben Reinhardt that contextualizes the lessons Pieces of the Action can offer to contemporary readers: that change depends both on heroic individuals and effective organizations; that a leader’s job is one of coordination; and that the path from idea to innovation is a long and winding one, inextricably bound to those involved—those enduring figures who have a piece of the action.\nL396: 【53†Purchase on Bookshop    $24†bookshop.org】 【54†Purchase on Barnes & Noble    $24†www.barnesandnoble.com】 【55†Purchase on Amazon    $24†www.amazon.com】\nL397: \nL398: Author\nL399: Vannevar Bush (1890–1974) was an engineer, inventor, educator, and architect of public-sector funding for scientific research in the United States. During World War II, he oversaw a wide-ranging R&D program that paired government funding with scientific research, resulting in the development of radar and the initiation of the Manhattan Project. Outside of government, he conducted foundational research in computer science, including the invention of the differential analyzer.\nL400: His 1945 essay “As We May Think” is credited with anticipating aspects of personal computing and the internet, while his government report Science, The Endless Frontier laid the groundwork for the founding of the National Science Foundation. Both are considered seminal texts that have inspired successive generations of scientists and engineers.\nL401: 【56†PDF†assets.stripeassets.com】\nL402: \nL403: Praise\nL404: \nL405: > Pieces of the Action is not just about science and innovation, it’s about state capacity. Anyone who longs for renewed, effective institutions will find the wisdom of a kindred spirit reflecting on how to build them.\nL406: \nL407: Eli Dourado\nL408: \nL409: economist and senior research fellow at the Center for Growth and Opportunity at Utah State University\nL410: > Pieces of the Action provides a window into the way Bush saw himself: as part of a larger cultural tradition, equipping new generations with knowledge from past ones. I came away encouraged and inspired by the knowledge that great things can be built in spite of stagnation, rigid norms, and conventions, through the determination and foresight of people who work to change institutions—and build new ones, too.\nL411: \nL412: Saloni Dattani\nL413: \nL414: cofounder and editor of Works in Progress and researcher at Our World in Data\nL415: > Scientific and technological progress depend crucially on leadership. Bush understood this better than anyone, and he had a front-row seat to the most important R&D work of the 20th century. This candid memoir gave me a better understanding of how research leadership works and why it’s desperately needed.\nL416: \nL417: Jason Crawford\nL418: \nL419: writer at The Roots of Progress\nL420: > It’s hard to deny that Vannevar Bush was at the nexus of an astounding number of people and events that shaped the modern world. In Pieces of the Action, he works hard to give us a window into his thought processes, and from them distill timeless lessons about leadership, research, institution building, and human nature.\nL421: \nL422: Ben Reinhardt\nL423: \nL424: CEO of PARPA, researcher at the Astera Institute, and host of the Idea Machines podcast\n"
